  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Increasing Mo from x = 0.2 to x = 1 in the CoCrFeNiMox-Ni/MoS2-Ag-Cr2O3 composite will reduce the coefficient of friction from room temperature to 800 °C.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Increasing Mo from x = 0.2 to x = 1 will reduce the wear rate over the stated service-temperature range.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Increasing Mo produces greater FCC lattice distortion.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Increasing Mo promotes formation of a hard, Mo-rich tetragonal sigma phase.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "High-temperature oxidation and tribochemical reactions form a continuous glaze containing layered silver molybdates and other oxides.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Dissolved Mo impedes dislocation motion through solid-solution strengthening.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The rigid sigma phase suppresses plastic deformation and scratching through second-phase strengthening.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "At lower temperatures, Ni/MoS2 provides a low-shear lubricating film.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "At lower temperatures, Ag reduces adhesion between the sliding surfaces.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "At high temperatures, the oxide/molybdate glaze separates the contacting surfaces.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Separation by the high-temperature glaze lowers interfacial shear and material removal.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Mo1 composite will have greater hardness than the Mo0.2 composite.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The friction and wear improvements from the dense oxide/molybdate glaze will be especially pronounced at 600–800 °C.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Mo0.2 baseline is predominantly an FCC solid solution with minimal sigma phase.",
      "field": "baseline",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Mo0.2 baseline has friction coefficients of 0.66 at room temperature, 0.75 at 200 °C, and 0.21 at 800 °C.",
      "field": "baseline",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Mo0.2 baseline has wear rates of 13.1 × 10^-6 mm3/(N·m) at room temperature, 98.1 × 10^-6 mm3/(N·m) at 400 °C, and 6.6 × 10^-6 mm3/(N·m) at 800 °C.",
